sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1 (an external API call is an effect, so it is a run), R2-R4, env_import vault pattern. docs/reference/conn.md#pairing-operations.
  %% status: migrated
  %% command: none · API (conn-pairing-create)
  %% kind: conn.pairing_create
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane T3 icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>API callers
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant T3 as T3 server<br/>T3 auth API
  CLI->>GW: Pair with env-beast<br/>POST conn/…/env-beast/pairings
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `POST /api/v1/conn/environments/env-beast/pairings`
  %%> - syntax: the MCP tool `conn-pairing-create` with `environment`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the registered T3 server; the calling Node, such as `phone`, is who gets paired
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - there is no `orbit` command
  Note over GW: Check the server<br/>task #5170 · session to vault
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the `conn` extension is on; the caller is an active Node; no access grant is needed
  %%> - env-beast is registered and the Gateway's admin session there has not expired, else `conn.session_expired`
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - records pairing 7 with a unique client label, such as `phone via Orbit k3j9x0qa`
  %%> - pushes the admin session to the Swamp vault as `conn-session-5170` after commit (R3)
  %%> Why a run:
  %%> - calling T3's API is an effect; the Gateway's only execution primitives are mint and sign
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5170 recorded with the full intent
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-conn-pairing-create
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the T3 server URL, the client label, and the vault key names; never the session
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5170
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the caller waits on the task and shows its progress; the run takes about a second
  Note over SW: Create private reply
  %%> How:
  %%> - mktemp creates a private directory for the T3 response
  SW->>T3: Mint a pairing link<br/>POST /api/auth/pairing-token
  %%> How:
  %%> - one HTTPS request from 209, with the admin session from `vault.get`
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - asks T3 for a one-time pairing token with T3's administrative scopes and the admitted client label
  Note over SW: Store the link in vault
  %%> How:
  %%> - a typed data method reads the private response as T3's pairing result: id, credential, expiry and the echoed label; it ignores added fields
  %%> - it publishes the credential as a native sensitive field
  %%> - its resource instance is unique to pairing 7, so concurrent pairings have different vault keys
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the credential to the native key for pairing 7 and returns only public metadata and its key name
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - the link never goes into step outputs, the run's result or a log
  Note over SW: Remove private reply
  %%> How:
  %%> - declared completed or skipped dependencies clean up after failure too
  Note over SW: Confirm pairing
  %%> Check:
  %%> - the native assert requires a stored sensitive output and successful cleanup
  alt T3 returned a link
    T3-->>SW: one-time token · 5 min
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the token and its expiry
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · expiry
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - only public metadata: the expiry
    Note over GW: Read the link once<br/>empty the key · task done
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads pairing 7's native credential key once, keeps the link encrypted on pairing 7, then deletes the key with the native `vault.delete` frame
    %%> Result:
    %%> - pairing 7 gets its issued and expiry times; task #5170 succeeds
  else T3 refuses or no reply
    T3--xSW: 401 or no answer
    %%> When:
    %%> - T3 refuses the admin session, refuses the call, answers with invalid data, or cannot be reached
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>conn.* error_code
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - the failed step's code: `conn.session_rejected` (401), `conn.request_refused` (another status), `conn.response_invalid` (2xx without T3's shape)
    %%> - any other failure, such as no answer, is `conn.pairing_outcome_unknown`
    Note over GW: Mark pairing 7 failed<br/>task failed
    %%> Result:
    %%> - pairing 7 is marked failed; task #5170 fails
    %%> On the T3 server:
    %%> - a link minted before a lost or invalid reply stays unused and expires within minutes; that is harmless
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - T3's answer is final: do not retry `session_rejected`, `request_refused` or `response_invalid` until the cause is fixed
    %%> - after `pairing_outcome_unknown`, ask again; it is a new task with a new label
  end
  loop until terminal
    CLI->>GW: Read Operation #5170<br/>GET conn/operations/5170
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the requester polls `GET /api/v1/conn/operations/5170` for status and public results
    GW-->>CLI: status + public result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - status and public pairing metadata; never the pairing URL
  end
  CLI->>GW: Read pairing 7 once<br/>GET conn/…/env-beast/pairings/7
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - after success, the caller reads `GET /api/v1/conn/environments/env-beast/pairings/7`
  GW-->>CLI: 200 OK · pairing_url
  %%> Returns:
  %%> - the owner-checked read returns the URL once and clears its encrypted credential
  %%> - a second read, an expired pairing or a read before completion returns 409
